                    r1_title:               `Round 1: Cultural Dimension Analysis`,
                    r1_briefing:            `<strong>Grupo Empresarial Catracho (GEC)</strong> is a Honduran conglomerate (USD 450M revenue) seeking a strategic partner for its telecom division (GEC-Telecom, USD 120M revenue).<br><br>Two candidates have emerged:<br>• <strong>Kumoshiro Tech (Japan)</strong> — USD 12B conglomerate proposing a 50-50 JV to create "Kumoshiro-GEC Latinoamerica"<br>• <strong>Marrowfield Technology (USA)</strong> — Private equity firm offering USD 250M to acquire GEC-Telecom outright<br><br>Before meeting either partner, CEO Roberto Martinez must prepare culturally. Using Hofstede dimensions:<br>• Honduras: High power distance (74), collectivist (37), high uncertainty avoidance (80), short-term (26)<br>• Japan: Formal hierarchy (54), collectivist (46), very high uncertainty avoidance (92), long-term (88)<br>• USA: Low power distance (40), individualist (91), low uncertainty avoidance (46), short-term (26)<br><br><strong>Decide:</strong> How much to invest in cultural preparation, and what communication style to adopt for initial meetings.`,
                    r2_title:               `Round 2: Partner Evaluation and Selection`,
                    r2_briefing:            `Roberto has completed his cultural preparation and is ready to choose a strategic direction.<br><br><strong>Option A — Kumoshiro Tech JV:</strong><br>• Deal structure: Joint Venture with shared ownership<br>• Valuation: ~USD 180M for JV entity<br>• Timeline: 6–12 months (relationship-building required)<br>• Upside: Regional growth, technology access, long-term value<br>• Risk: High cultural friction, slow decision-making<br><br><strong>Option B — Marrowfield Acquisition:</strong><br>• Deal structure: Full acquisition (100% sale)<br>• Price: USD 250M cash<br>• Timeline: 3 months (standard due diligence)<br>• Upside: Immediate liquidity, clean exit<br>• Risk: Loss of control, no future upside<br><br><strong>Decide:</strong> Which partner to pursue, and if Kumoshiro, what equity split to propose.`,
                    r3_title:               `Round 3: Crisis — Cultural Repair`,
                    r3_briefing:            `Roberto traveled to Tokyo for the first meeting with Kumoshiro Tech. Several cultural errors occurred:<br><br>• Arrived 10 minutes late (disrespectful in Japan)<br>• Gave an overly firm handshake to President Yamamoto (70 years old)<br>• Immediately presented aggressive proposals (too direct)<br>• Toasted with whisky before the host (protocol breach)<br>• Did not bring gifts for the Kumoshiro team (expected in Japanese culture)<br>• Pushed for a decision in one meeting (Japanese expect 5-6 meetings over months)<br><br>Yamamoto privately told his team: "Too direct, no respect for hierarchy, very Western." His operations director considers the 50-50 JV proposal an insult given that Kumoshiro is 26x larger than GEC.<br><br><strong>Decide:</strong> How to repair the relationship — budget for gifts and cultural advisors, how much time to concede, and whether to adjust equity terms.`,
                    r4_title:               `Round 4: JV Governance Design`,
                    r4_briefing:            `The relationship has been partially repaired. Now the teams must design the governance structure for the Joint Venture (or finalize acquisition terms with Marrowfield).<br><br>Key governance elements to decide:<br><br>• <strong>Board composition:</strong> How many of 10 seats go to GEC vs. the partner? Should reflect equity split.<br>• <strong>CEO rotation:</strong> Partners alternate appointing the JV CEO. How long each term? (Japan prefers stability; 3 years is typical)<br>• <strong>Conflict resolution:</strong> Litigation is adversarial (bad for Japanese culture). Arbitration is neutral. Mediation emphasizes harmony (preferred by Japanese partners).<br><br>Good governance design respects cultural differences and builds long-term institutional trust.<br><br><strong>Decide:</strong> Board seats, CEO rotation period, and conflict resolution method.`
